<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Weissenau</h1><p class="st-meta">1257 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Ravensburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Premonstratensian abbey near Ravensburg that ruled a small Swabian territory as an imperial prelacy and built a grand baroque church.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The abbey was founded in 1145 by Gebizo of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-ravensburg">Ravensburg</a>, a ministerial of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a>, and settled by Premonstratensian canons. It lay on the Schussen just south of Ravensburg. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> and later royal protection it gained imperial immediacy in the 13th century, around 1257.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbots, elected by the canons, ruled villages, farms and forests around the abbey and sat on the bench of Swabian imperial prelates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. Their territory was small and rural, and they defended their rights against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-ravensburg">Free Imperial City of Ravensburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> bailiwick. In the early 18th century the abbey rebuilt its church in baroque style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey was secularised in 1803 and given to the counts of Sternberg-Manderscheid as compensation for lands lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in 1806 it passed to Württemberg. Its church survives as a parish church in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-ravensburg">Ravensburg</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1257 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.72%;width:10.87%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1257 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ravensburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Weißenau Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1530&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-weissenau" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Weissenau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4904 504 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Weissenau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5224 760L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ravensburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1530&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-weissenau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

